# Viz Vera — Cross-Project Experience

Timeless visualization patterns. Any future Vera dispatched on any project should read this first.

## Wave 10H.1 — Disposition/Exploration Rules + Backfill Pitfalls (2026-04-22)

- **VIZ-O1 (disposition) + VIZ-E1 (exploration zone) went live.** "Orphan" charts (no consumer code reference) are NOT auto-candidates for deletion — their correct home is the Exploration Zone with an ELI5 caption. Chart-deletion recommendations based purely on absence of consumer references are unsafe; this caution was first surfaced in Wave 10F cross-review and is now codified.
- **Authored 3 ELI5 captions** for Sample-pair orphans under VIZ-E1.
- **Sidecar backfill lesson:** match legacy sidecars by **file stem**, NOT by the `chart_name` JSON field. Old sidecars carry a prefix inconsistency (pair-prefixed internal names vs bare-name filenames post-Wave-10F), so `chart_name`-based joins miss files. File-stem matching is the robust key.
- **Generator consolidation gap (BL-VIZ-SIDECAR-HELPER):** only 3 of 7 pair-generators route through a sidecar-writer helper; the other 4 use bare `fig.write_json()` and bypass palette/audience/disposition metadata entirely. Any future sidecar rule change requires first landing the shared helper, then migrating the 4 bypass generators — patching one generator at a time leaves silent drift.

## Bare-Name Migration Pattern (2026-04-22)

When migrating chart files from pair-prefixed to bare-name convention:
1. Pre-check for conflicts with `ls | grep -v "^{prefix}_"` — if any bare-name files already exist that would collide, STOP.
2. Use `git mv` for all renames (JSON + _meta.json sidecars in the same loop pass).
3. After rename, grep consumer code for remaining prefixed references — CSV download filenames and doc path strings are NOT chart loader references and can be safely ignored.
4. Run smoke_loader on the renamed pair PLUS any other pairs in scope to confirm no cross-pair regressions.
5. Three chart types (`hero_spread_vs_spy`, `spread_history_annotated`, `tournament_sharpe_dist`) had zero consumer code references on hy_ig_v2_spy — flagged as "rename-and-flag" for a future deletion audit rather than deleted in the same dispatch.

## Palette Discipline

- **Canonical palette:** Okabe-Ito 2026, authoritative at `docs/schemas/color_palette_registry.json`. Never use matplotlib defaults (`#d62728`, `#1f77b4`, `#2ca02c`, `#ff7f0e`) as trace colors — they look sloppy next to the palette primaries and fail the VIZ-V11 pre-save lint.
- **Role keys, not hex values:** primary data trace `#D55E00`, secondary `#0072B2`, tertiary `#009E73`, NBER shading `rgba(150,120,120,0.22)`, event-marker line `#4D4D4D`. Reference by role in builder scripts; consumers (narrative, captions) can say "the vermillion line" knowing it is always the primary role.
- **Every chart records `palette_id` in `_meta.json`.** Missing `palette_id` fails the VIZ-V5 smoke test. Palette changes are methodological divergence under META-XVC and need a regression_note entry.

## NBER Shading and Shape Perceptibility

- **Alpha floor is 0.20–0.28, not 0.10–0.15.** A prior rule prescribed 0.1–0.15 and shipped imperceptible shading that looked identical to the plot background. Any numeric visual prescription MUST be perceptually validated — treat the number in the rule as a hypothesis, not a guarantee.
- **Subplot coverage is per-panel.** A `layout.Shape` with `xref='x'` hits only the first subplot. For dual-panel zooms (VIZ-V1) emit one shape per panel per recession — total `n_recessions × n_panels`. Same rule for event-marker vertical lines.
- **Perceptual validation (META-PV, mandatory before handoff):** after saving a chart's JSON, render it to a PNG via `plotly.io.from_json` + kaleido and save as `_perceptual_check_{chart_name}.png`. If the shading / annotation / encoding is not clearly visible at standard zoom, the chart fails completeness.

## Canonical Contracts (single source of truth)

- **Chart-type registry (VIZ-V8):** `docs/schemas/chart_type_registry.json` is the SINGLE SOURCE for the method → chart_type mapping. No partial copies in SOP prose — only references. Any new method requires a registry PR before any chart production; bumping `x-version` is mandatory.
- **Events registry (VIZ-V12):** historical-episode event markers come from `docs/schemas/history_zoom_events_registry.json`. Every event carries `rationale` + `source_citation` (NBER, FOMC, SEC, BLS, primary news wire — never agent discretion). Ad-hoc date picks are prohibited.
- **Annotation strategy registry (VIZ-V13):** choose exactly one named strategy — `descending_stair` (timeline events), `top_right_uniform` (corner-anchored), `alternating_top_bottom` (dense 5+ markers). Record `annotation_strategy_id` in `_meta.json`. `manual_override` is allowed but carries a regression_note obligation.

## Abstraction Layer Discipline (META-AL)

- **Rendered charts are per-pair; never canonical.** A rendered chart embeds the pair's indicator and target series, so it cannot be shared across pairs. The canonical layer is metadata only — events, palette, rules. Do NOT emit `output/_comparison/history_zoom_*.json` — that model was removed.
- **Dual-panel zooms are mandatory.** Single-panel zooms that show only the indicator strip the pair-relationship that is the entire narrative point. Top panel = indicator, bottom panel = target, shared x-axis. Event markers and NBER shading span BOTH panels.

## Per-Method Ownership (VIZ-V3, VIZ-V4)

- **No silent fallbacks.** Every method has its own canonical chart. Granger's F-by-lag bar chart is NOT the same file as Local Projections IRF. If an artifact is missing, emit a GATE-25 "chart pending" placeholder — never silently serve a lookalike from another method.
- **No silent drops.** Mandatory diagnostics per method (CCF → pre-whitened + Q1–Q4 returns; Regime → prob timeline + quartile returns; Quantile → coefficient plot; Granger → F-by-lag; TE → TE-by-lag). Removing any requires a regression_note entry citing why.

## End-to-End Smoke Test (VIZ-V5)

- Before handoff, iterate over every chart: `plotly.io.read_json()` must succeed, `len(fig.data) > 0`, `fig.layout.title.text` must be non-empty. Log pass/fail per chart to `_smoke_test_{YYYYMMDD}.log`. Any FAIL is a blocker.
- Smoke-test catches structural integrity; perceptual-check catches visual legibility; A3 catches spec conformance. Three different failure modes, three different checks.

## Chart-Text Coherence (GATE-24 / SL-3)

- When updating chart data, labels, or semantics, `grep` all narrative files (`app/pages/*`, `docs/portal_narrative_*.md`, `content/*_narrative.py`) for captions referencing the chart. Notify Ray in the SAME dispatch so the caption lands in the same commit. Silent chart updates that leave narrative stale are a cross-agent coordination failure.

## Filename Canon (VIZ-A3, VIZ-NM1)

- Portal Plotly JSON lives at `output/charts/{pair_id}/plotly/{chart_type}.json`. The `pair_id` appears in the directory path ONLY, never in the filename. Ace's loader calls `load_plotly_chart("{chart_type}", pair_id="{pair_id}")` — the canonical filename must match that literal string bit-for-bit.

---

## 2026-04-11 — Filename conventions must match loader calls exactly

When a convention crosses an agent boundary, write the convention as the EXACT string the consumer will use, not a description. Grep the consumer's code for the literal call site and match it.

## 2026-04-11 — Caption ownership: display vs audit are separate fields

Ray writes display captions (audience-facing). Vera writes technical captions in `_meta.json` (machine-readable audit trail, used as fallback). Without explicit ownership they silently duplicate or disagree.

## 2026-04-11 — Unit audits live inside the chart script, not in review

Put `assert max(y) >= 10 if label == "bps"` at save-time. Don't rely on stakeholder review to catch unit mislabels.

## 2026-04-19 — Numeric rule prescriptions must be perceptually validated

A prescribed alpha (0.10–0.15) shipped imperceptible shading. Rules that encode visual perception thresholds need a render-and-eyeball step, not just conformance to the number.

---

### Wave 10F cross-review — 2026-04-22 — Filename convention enforcement gap

The bare-name chart filename rule (VIZ-NM1 / VIZ-A3 / VIZ-V8) was declared in the SOP but had zero producer-side enforcement. Two full pairs (indpro_xlp, umcsent_xlv) shipped entirely in the prefixed dialect the rule prohibits, and no gate caught it because Ace's loader-side smoke test only runs on pages Ace has authored — and those pairs had no portal pages yet. Pattern: a consumer-side contract check does not substitute for a producer-side check; deferred consumption = delayed detection. Fix requires a pre-commit `check_chart_filename.sh` that asserts every `output/charts/*/plotly/*.json` basename matches a `canonical_filename_pattern` in the chart_type_registry.

### Wave 10F cross-review — 2026-04-22 — Sidecar type distinction is load-bearing

Chart sidecars (`_meta.json`) and dataset sidecars (`_manifest.json`) describe different object types with minimal field overlap. Merging them under one name creates an ambiguous schema that either overloads both field sets or forces consumers to branch on artifact type anyway. The distinction preserves a semantic cue: `_meta.json` is descriptive metadata (what the content means); `_manifest.json` is a loading contract with assertions (what files are here, how to load them). Keep them distinct. When writing a rule that touches sidecars, verify which artifact type you are describing before naming the file — one drafting slip in VIZ-IC1 §6 (`_manifest.json` where `_meta.json` was intended) created an apparent conflict and triggered a full cross-review.

### Wave 10F cross-review — 2026-04-22 — Two-level semantic/visual role split in palette registry

Chart scripts need visual-encoding role keys (`primary_data_trace`, `equity_curve`, …) to pick colors. Audit rules (VIZ-IC1 §4) need semantic role keys (`indicator`, `target`, `benchmark`) to assert the right data series got the right color. Both levels are load-bearing. The right architecture is semantic aliases that map onto existing visual-encoding keys — additive, not disruptive — rather than rewriting either the chart scripts or the audit rule. Aliases also follow the pattern of other portal registries (`signal_code_registry.json`, `caption_prefix_vocab.json`) that already have semantic→canonical indirection. When adding a new audit assertion that references a domain concept (indicator, target, benchmark), add an alias first; do not force chart scripts to adopt domain vocabulary or audit rules to adopt visual-encoding vocabulary.

---
*Last updated: 2026-04-22 (Wave 10F cross-review promotion)*

## Wave 10F Migration — 2026-04-22 — Consumer-side references must also be updated

When renaming chart files to bare-name canonical form, the producer-side `git mv` is only half the work. Consumer files (portal pages, pair configs, smoke_loader registries) contain the old chart name strings as literals. If not updated in the same commit, smoke tests fail immediately. Pattern: filename migration = `git mv` + grep every consumer file for old name + sed-replace + verify. The file-system rename alone does not propagate to call sites.

## Wave 10F Migration — 2026-04-22 — Sidecar creation at migration time, not separately

When renaming or adding bare-name chart files, create `_meta.json` sidecars in the same pass. Doing it as a separate wave costs two commit cycles and risks a window where charts exist without sidecars. The sidecar content (title from `layout.title.text`, `palette_id`, `rules_applied`) is derivable programmatically from the chart JSON alone — no human input needed for retro-apply.

---

## Wave 10J/10K — Commit Without Push Is Not a Deliverable (2026-04-24)

In Wave 10J/10K, agents committed chart and portal changes but did not immediately push. Streamlit Cloud reads from the remote, so the app remained stale and two cloud reboots were wasted before the missing pushes were identified. A commit that exists only locally is invisible to every downstream consumer. Rule: always `git push origin main` in the same step as `git commit` — the push is the delivery, not the commit. This is now codified as META-CPD in team-coordination.md and cross-referenced in every agent SOP.

**How to apply:** After any `git commit`, immediately run `git push origin main` before writing a handoff note or declaring the task done.

## Wave 10J/10K — History Zoom Episode Completeness Gap (2026-04-24)

**What happened:** `history_zoom_{slug}.json` files were silently absent for 8 of 10 pairs on the portal. Rules VIZ-ZOOM1 and VIZ-V1 defined when and how to produce zoom charts but contained no mechanical verification step before handoff. Dispatches completed without error; the Story-page crisis section was simply empty.

**Failure mode class:** "SOP says produce X, but no gate verifies X was produced before handoff." This is the same class as the NBER shading gap (VIZ-NBER1, Wave 10J) — a rule that lives in prose but has no binding check at the handoff boundary. Prose rules are intentions; gates are enforcement.

**Fix:** VIZ-HZE1 — mandatory `git ls-files output/charts/{pair_id}/plotly/history_zoom_{slug}.json` check for every required slug before any Ace handoff. Skipped slugs (data coverage gap) must be documented in `_meta.json` with a structured skip entry. Gate result must appear verbatim in the handoff note.

**Key lesson:** Every "Vera produces chart type X" rule must have a paired "Vera verifies chart type X is on disk before handoff" gate. Structural checks (VIZ-CV1 smoke) do not substitute — they only verify files that exist; they cannot detect files that should exist but don't. Production absence requires an enumeration-and-check gate, not a structural-integrity gate.

## Wave 10J HZE1 Retro — Dual-Panel Axis Assignment Bug (2026-04-24)

**Failure mode: "data present in JSON ≠ data visible on screen."**

29 `history_zoom` charts shipped from the HZE1 retro-apply with the bottom-panel target trace assigned `xaxis="x"` instead of `xaxis="x2"`. Because `yaxis2` is anchored to `x2`, the lower panel rendered with correct y-axis labels and tick values but a completely blank line — 800+ data points existed in the JSON but were invisible on screen. No data-existence check (VIZ-HZE1 `git ls-files`), no structural smoke (VIZ-CV1 `len(fig.data) > 0`), and no JSON-level inspection could have detected this. Only visual inspection of a rendered chart would catch it.

**Why it slipped through:** VIZ-CV1 mandated kaleido perceptual renders for hero and equity-curve charts, but NOT for `history_zoom` charts. If a kaleido PNG had been generated for even one zoom chart, the blank bottom panel would have been immediately visible.

**New rule:** VIZ-DP1 — Dual-Panel Axis Assignment Verification. Mandates: (1) programmatic axis-assignment check script on every dual-panel chart JSON before handoff; (2) kaleido perceptual render MANDATORY for all `history_zoom` charts (extended from VIZ-CV1); (3) visual inspection of both panels non-blank before commit.

**Key principle to internalize:** JSON structural validity and data presence are necessary but not sufficient for visual correctness. Dual-panel charts introduce a coordinate-system mapping that is invisible to all data-layer checks. The only reliable catch is rendering. Any new dual-panel chart type requires a perceptual render gate — this is not optional even when the chart type is "simple."

**Gate text to paste in handoff notes:**
```
VIZ-DP1 gate — {pair_id}: PASS / FAIL [list violations]
VIZ-DP1 perceptual render — {pair_id}/{slug}: both panels non-blank, NBER visible, event markers span both panels.
```

## Wave 10J Lessons (2026-04-24)

### Pattern: NBER shading without a mandatory chart-type list produces drift
Rule V2 said "long-horizon charts must have NBER shading" but without an explicit mandatory list, individual judgment at chart-creation time produced 5/10 hero charts missing shading and all rolling charts unshaded. VIZ-NBER1 now provides the explicit list — follow it mechanically, not by judgment.

### Pattern: Missing trigger rule for zoom charts causes inconsistency
8/10 pairs had zero zoom charts because no rule defined when they were required. VIZ-ZOOM1 now provides the three-condition trigger (data coverage + domain relevance + portal delivery). Apply it at pair-creation time, not after the fact.

### Pattern: CP chart types need canonical specs before Evan delivers
When ECON-CP1/CP2 results arrive, Vera needs VIZ-CP1 specs already in the SOP — not ad-hoc decisions at delivery time. VIZ-CP1 now covers all five chart types with NBER shading requirements and layout specs.

## 2026-04-24 — Wave 10J/10K Self-Reflection

### What went well
- Debate contributions were evidence-grounded: live role-boundary violation in VIZ-NBER1, shared-script gap in ECON-DIR1/RES-OD1, direction-vocabulary seam — all cited with rule IDs and wave references.
- Self-assessment produced accurate defect mapping before being prompted: 5 hero charts missing NBER, 9 other mandatory charts missing, 34 zoom charts missing across 8 pairs.
- Canonical contract architecture (VIZ-V8/V12/V13) held across 10 pairs — zero undocumented annotation strategies or off-palette colors.

### NBER shading gap root cause
Rule V2 existed since Wave 3, but pair pipeline scripts were forked from a pre-Wave 3 original. Hand-crafted charts (HY-IG) were compliant; pipeline-generated charts were not. Treated "rule exists in SOP" as equivalent to "rule is implemented in all code paths." Those are not the same thing. Fix: VIZ-V2 must be enforced at generation time in the chart helper function, not post-hoc via audit.

### Role boundary violation root cause
Only caught VIZ-NBER1 prescribing Quincy's verification method because the debate format forced reading own SOP as external reviewer. Normal production dispatches don't create that perspective — which means violations persist indefinitely without structured review.

### Perceptual PNG carry-forward
Three compounding failures: kaleido 0.2.x WARNs treated as cosmetic; no blocking downstream gate; deferred as "separate track" without assigning an agent or wave. Will not close until Ace upgrades kaleido and Quincy adds PNG existence to GATE-27.

### Walk-forward redesign debate
Redesign with continuous datetime axes is architecturally correct but blocked on Evan's fold-level oos_start_date/oos_end_date output. Track as cross-agent dependency on Evan, not a Vera backlog item.

### Key lessons to carry forward
1. "Rule exists in SOP" ≠ "rule is implemented in all code paths." Audit pipeline scripts at rule-authoring time.
2. Blocking gates must live in production code, not in memory or SOP text.
3. Kaleido deprecation warnings on mandatory checks are compliance gaps, not cosmetic issues.

## 2026-06-12 — Plotly fill-band traces silently render default-palette markers

A `go.Scatter` CI-band trace (`fill="toself"`, `line=dict(width=0)`) without an explicit `mode="lines"` renders markers at every band vertex in Plotly's default cycle colors (purple/teal). The palette-conformance lint cannot catch it because `marker.color` is None — the color is resolved at render time. Two charts (local_projections, quantile_coef) shipped through VIZ-IC1 clean and were caught only by the perceptual-PNG eyeball.

**How to apply:** always set `mode="lines"` on fill-band traces; treat "lint passed but render-time defaults exist" as a known blind spot the perceptual check covers.

