# Viz Vera — Key Memories

## Wave 10H.1 generator-bypass incident (2026-04-22)
- Dispatched to backfill VIZ-O1/E1 sidecar metadata across pair generators. Discovered 4 of 7 pair-generators use bare `fig.write_json()` with no sidecar writer — they silently bypass any sidecar-level rule (palette_id, audience_tier, disposition, exploration caption). Only 3 generators had a sidecar-writer to patch.
- Consequence: patching the 3 routed generators left the other 4 as silent-drift sources. Filed `BL-VIZ-SIDECAR-HELPER` to introduce a shared sidecar helper before any further sidecar-schema change.
- Secondary: legacy sidecar backfill must key off **file stem**, not the `chart_name` JSON field, because Wave 10F bare-name migration left pair-prefixed `chart_name` values inside otherwise-renamed sidecars.
- Cross-check with Wave 10F: orphan charts are NOT deletion candidates — VIZ-E1 (exploration zone) is their correct home. Confirmed this during Wave 10F cross-review and again in Wave 10H.1.

## Wave 10F bare-name migration (2026-04-22)
- Completed the deferred 12-chart rename on `hy_ig_v2_spy`. All 24 files (JSON + meta) renamed via `git mv`. No conflicts existed. Smoke: 15/0, 8/0, 7/0. Commit 27fb01f.
- 3 charts flagged for next-wave deletion audit: `hero_spread_vs_spy`, `spread_history_annotated`, `tournament_sharpe_dist` — zero consumer code references in pages or components.
- Ace can now remove the loader fallback (Wave 10F item 6).

## Gotchas

- Evan's coefficient table column names vary across model types (statsmodels vs. linearmodels produce "coef" vs. "Coef." vs. "parameter"). Always check column names before building automated chart code; never assume a fixed schema. The SOP now specifies a standardized schema: `variable`, `coef`, `se`, `t_stat`, `p_value`, `ci_lower`, `ci_upper`.
- Chart specifications from upstream arrive as informal messages, not structured requests. Budget extra time for clarification or proactively ask for: chart type, key message, variables to highlight, and annotation needs. The SOP now includes a structured Acknowledgment Template to close this loop.
- As the last agent in the pipeline, upstream delays compress my time disproportionately. When I see Research or Data handoffs running late, start on anything I can do in parallel (template setup, style config, exploratory plots from available data) rather than waiting idle.
- Research briefs contain event dates and regime boundaries buried in prose. Scan the full brief myself rather than assuming Evan will relay annotation-relevant details. The SOP now formalizes a Research-to-Viz annotation pathway.
- Never produce a chart from data you have not sanity-checked. A quick validation on intake (date range, gaps, plausible values) catches handoff errors before they become misleading charts.
- Plotly JSON (`.json`) is the only safe serialization format for handing Plotly figures to Ace. Do NOT use pickle for `go.Figure` objects -- same version-coupling risk I worry about with Evan's model pickles. Do NOT use `.html` -- Ace cannot embed it cleanly in Streamlit.
- When portal assembly is in scope, every chart needs an audience tier tag and portal page mapping. Without these, Ace has to guess where charts go, which causes misplacement and rework.

## Insights

- Dana's data dictionary is my most important input document, not Evan's model results. Without understanding the data (units, transformations, display names), I cannot produce a chart title that states the finding. Treat a missing data dictionary as a blocker, not an inconvenience.
- Evan cannot always give me interpretation notes because he is under pressure to deliver rigor (diagnostics, sensitivity). When interpretation notes are thin, read his narrative paragraphs carefully — the finding is usually there, just not formatted as a chart title. Extract it rather than asking him to rewrite.
- The formal pipeline (Research -> Data -> Econ -> Viz) does not capture all real workflows. The updated SOP now formalizes three pathways: Econ-to-Viz (primary), Data-to-Viz (direct, for exploratory/data quality charts), and Research-to-Viz (annotation context).
- My SOP was weak on specifying what I require from upstream. The "Inputs I Need" section per chart type now gives teammates concrete checklists.
- With Ace on the team, my outputs are now intermediate products, not final deliverables. This changes my quality calculus: charts must be integration-ready (serializable, tagged, documented), not just visually correct.
- The metadata sidecar file (`_meta.json`) is the key innovation for the Viz-to-App handoff. It carries caption, source, palette_id, annotation_strategy_id, events_registry_version, audience tier, portal page, and interactive controls hints.

## Incidents

### Wave 3 (2026-04-19) — Imperceptible NBER shading on HY-IG v2 hero

The hero chart shipped with NBER shading at grey-alpha-0.12 — the old VIZ-V2 rule prescribed 0.1–0.15 and I followed it. Stakeholder review (SL-2) caught that the shading was imperceptible against the Streamlit off-white background. Two bugs stacked: (1) the alpha was too low, and (2) the shading shape used `xref='x'` only, so in the dual-panel hero only the TOP panel got shaded — the bottom panel was bare.

**Fixes:** Rule V2 revised — alpha raised to 0.20–0.28, fillcolor pinned to `rgba(150,120,120,0.22)` (faded red-brown, distinct from background), and a subplot rule added: emit one shape per xaxis per recession (total = `n_recessions × n_panels`). Added **META-PV (perceptual validation)** — every chart produces a `_perceptual_check_{name}.png` PNG that a human confirms before handoff. A numeric rule that looked reasonable in the abstract shipped an invisible artifact; the lesson is that prescriptions like "alpha 0.1–0.15" are hypotheses, not guarantees, and must be validated visually.

