app/pair_configs/petrol_inv_spy_config.py:81:Out-of-sample (OOS) -- tested on data not used to pick the rule -- is the right lens here. The winning petroleum-inventory rule earns a Sharpe ratio -- return per unit of volatility -- of 1.48 versus 0.93 for buy-and-hold (buy-and-hold means staying invested in SPY throughout). Its maximum drawdown -- the largest peak-to-trough loss -- is -6.3% versus -23.9% for buy-and-hold. That sounds useful, but it is not a clean alpha story: annualized return is lower, 9.8% versus 15.2%.
app/pair_configs/petrol_inv_spy_config.py:83:The natural prior is counter-cyclical -- inventories building when demand is weak, which is usually bad for equities. That prior is visible in the Global Financial Crisis (GFC) and coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) windows: petroleum stocks rose as fuel demand weakened. The empirical result overturns that prior for the searched rule. The quartile chart shows Q1, the lowest 3-month petroleum-stock change, at Sharpe 0.37 and 6.0% annualized return; Q4, the highest 3-month change, at Sharpe 1.25 and 17.5%. That monotonic gradient corroborates a procyclical -- moving with the equity cycle -- interpretation.
