app/pair_configs/busloans_spy_config.py:212:After 6,100 strategy combinations were searched (4,396 passing validity filters), the best rule found was: **be long the S&P 500 (SPY) only when month-over-month loan growth — observed with a 6-month delay — is in the bottom quartile of its trailing 36-month range; otherwise hold cash.** In the search window (2018-02 to 2026-05) that rule produced a Sharpe ratio of 1.50 versus 0.89 for buy-and-hold, with a maximum drawdown of just −1.0% versus −23.9%.
app/pair_configs/busloans_spy_config.py:218:- The rule looked unimpressive on pre-2018 data (in-sample Sharpe 0.35), its edge is concentrated in one episode (the COVID recovery), and it was in the market only 25% of the time.
app/pair_configs/busloans_spy_config.py:224:- **Do not use C&I loan growth as an early-warning signal** — in four major drawdowns since 2000 it warned of none of them; investors needing advance warning should look to leading credit measures such as the high-yield spread (covered in the HY-IG pair) instead.
app/pair_configs/busloans_spy_config.py:227:- **The strategy's profile is drawdown-avoidance, not return-seeking** — it gave up 4.2 percentage points of annual return versus buy-and-hold (10.7% vs 14.8%) in exchange for a 23-point smaller maximum drawdown; investors whose priority is compounding total return would have been better off holding the index.
